     6	 * it (`scripts/gateway/pin-arsenal.mjs`). B36 closed on the promise that the
     7	 * construction engine is GENERATED from this repository's own files; that one
     8	 * step broke it. The same `pnpm construction:seed` on another machine, or on
     9	 * this one with a server down, built a DIFFERENT bench, and every count taken
    10	 * against it measured the workstation as much as the code.
    11	 *
    12	 * The corpus now lives in `db/seed/tool-pins.manifest.json`. The live
    13	 * enumeration becomes a hand-run refresh (`scripts/gateway/refresh-pin-manifest.ts`)
    14	 * that WRITES that file — never a thing the seed depends on.
    15	 *
    16	 * Two rules the shape below exists to keep:
    17	 *
    18	 *   - `dxb-mcp` is NOT in the manifest, on purpose. Its declarations are this
    19	 *     repository's own source, read in-process over an in-memory transport; a
    20	 *     file copy of them would go stale the moment a tool changed, and the seed
    21	 *     would pin the OLD hash — which the daily drift check then quarantines.
    22	 *     A manifest carrying dxb-mcp is refused, loudly, rather than seeded.
    23	 *
    24	 *   - a manifest is verified BEFORE a single row is written: every stored hash
    25	 *     is recomputed with the production hash function (`computeToolHash`, the
    26	 *     same one the gateway pins and checks with), a `(server, tool)` may appear
    27	 *     only once, and the counts the file states about itself must be the rows it
    28	 *     carries. Any of those throws naming the offending `server.tool` — seeding
    29	 *     something else quietly is the failure this row exists to end.
    30	 *
    31	 * WHAT VERIFICATION CANNOT SEE, measured 2026-09-21 and written here rather than
    32	 * implied away: `computeToolHash` hashes description + inputSchema, NOT the tool
    33	 * NAME (`packages/gateway/src/pin-check.ts`, production's own rule, untouched by
    34	 * this row). So a manifest whose `git_add` entry was RENAMED by hand — body and
    35	 * hash left agreeing — verifies clean and pins under the new name. Offline there
    36	 * is nothing left to compare it against; the defence is the other half of this
    37	 * pair, `pnpm construction:pins:check`, where a rename shows as one
    38	 * NOT IN THE MANIFEST and one MISSING FROM THE MACHINE. Duplicates and count
    39	 * lies are caught here; a rename is caught there.
    40	 *
    41	 * The serialization is deterministic — servers and tools sorted, object keys
    42	 * sorted recursively, two-space indent, trailing newline, and NO timestamp:
    43	 * the history belongs to git, and a second refresh on an unchanged machine must
    44	 * produce a byte-identical file.
